repository-code: 'https://github.com/rajmodi262/SynthProof'
abstract: >-
  SynthProof generates differentially private synthetic tabular data and
  reports both a formal upper bound on privacy loss and an empirical lower
  bound recovered by canary auditing, with every operation that reads the
  sensitive table charged to a privacy accountant and recorded in a signed
  append-only ledger. Its principal finding is negative and concerns the
  measurement rather than the mechanisms: the epsilon a one-run canary audit
  can certify is bounded by the canary count alone, so certifying a given
  epsilon costs on the order of ln(1/alpha)*e^epsilon canaries. At the counts
  used here the instrument could not have certified the epsilons being
  claimed, regardless of how much the mechanisms leaked. Empirical auditing
  is therefore a tool for catching broken implementations rather than for
  confirming tight bounds.
keywords:
  - differential privacy
  - synthetic data
  - privacy auditing
  - membership inference
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
